Write-up for PA3
----------------


Implementation Overview
-----------------------

The COOL parser was implemented using GNU Bison in cool.y.

The grammar recognizes class declarations, inheritance, attributes,
methods, formal parameters, arithmetic and comparison expressions,
assignments, control-flow expressions, method dispatch, object creation,
let expressions, case expressions, and blocks.

Semantic actions construct the COOL abstract syntax tree (AST) using
the constructors provided by the assignment framework.

Design Decisions
----------------

Operator precedence and associativity were explicitly declared to
resolve ambiguities and preserve the intended interpretation of COOL
expressions.

Lists of classes, features, formals, arguments, block expressions,
and case branches are constructed using the corresponding AST list
constructors.

Multiple let declarations are represented as nested let expressions,
preserving their original declaration order and scope.

Source line tracking uses Bison locations and the node_lineno
mechanism. Missing initialization expressions are represented by
no_expr() nodes with line number zero, matching the reference parser.

Syntax Error Recovery
---------------------

Error recovery productions were implemented for:

- Class declarations
- Class features (attributes and methods)
- Let declarations, synchronizing at commas to retain subsequent bindings
  and at IN for an invalid final binding
- Block expressions, synchronizing at semicolons or the closing brace

These productions use Bison's error token and synchronization
delimiters to continue parsing after syntax errors.

Previously recognized AST elements are preserved where appropriate.
Invalid programs are rejected after their errors have been reported.
Recovery at a let comma resets the error status before parsing the next
binding so consecutive invalid bindings each receive a diagnostic.
A malformed block recovered at its closing brace keeps earlier valid
expressions when available, or uses no_expr() as a placeholder. Recovery
placeholders are never emitted as a successful program AST.

Testing and Validation
----------------------

good.cl contains valid COOL programs covering the implemented
language constructs, including nested expressions, operator
precedence, dispatch, let declarations, and case expressions.

bad.cl contains invalid programs covering malformed classes,
attributes, methods, let declarations, and block expressions.

The parser was built and the test files were run through myparser in the
configured COOL environment. The captured outputs were inspected:

- good.output contains one _program root and all 15 classes, without
  syntax errors. It is identical to the previously versioned output.
- bad.output contains 21 syntax diagnostics and the expected final
  "Compilation halted due to lex and parse errors" message.
- The diagnostics include both invalid bindings in the same let,
  consecutive invalid bindings, and malformed blocks closed without
  a semicolon, including a nested block. Parsing continues to the final
  intentionally incomplete class.

These checks confirm the observed behavior of the supplied tests.
A fresh comparison against the official reference parser has not been
performed in this verification; equality with a previously saved output
alone is not evidence of equality with the reference implementation.

To reproduce the tests, enter the configured container from the root:

    docker compose run --rm cool

Then run inside the container:

    cd PA3
    make parser
    make dotest

To capture each test output explicitly, run from PA3:

    ./myparser good.cl > good.output 2>&1
    ./myparser bad.cl > bad.output 2>&1

The negative test is expected to return an error status. Python is not
required for these commands. PA3/lexer should point to the official
assignment lexer when validating for submission.

Block comments before each class in good.cl describe its test purpose.
Comments in bad.cl identify the deliberate errors and recovery cases.
Regenerate good.output after changing comment layout so AST source line
numbers match the current input.

Bison conflict reports were checked during development, and the
final grammar produced no reported parsing conflicts in the tests.

Conclusion
----------

The implemented parser successfully recognizes the tested valid
COOL constructions, generates the expected AST, and provides
syntax error recovery for the tested invalid constructions.

The inspected outputs support the implementation for the supplied test
cases; they do not guarantee acceptance of every unseen test.
